%T A400334 9,4,3,2,9,1,1,4,5,8,5,4,0,0,4,4,8,7,8,9,8,1,5,4,7,2,5,4,4,0,7,4,9,3,
%U A400334 0,8,0,1,3,6,9,2,3,3,3,8,5,3,0,0,7,0,4
%N A400334 Decimal expansion of (3/2)*exp(-6^(1/3)).
%C A400334 For distributions P and Q on {0,...,n}, total variation distance is TV(P,Q) = (1/2) * Sum_{j=0..n} |P(j)-Q(j)|. Here Hyp(N,K,n) denotes the hypergeometric distribution for n draws without replacement from N objects, K of which are marked. A binomial mixture is a distribution of the form int_0^1 Bin(n,p) mu(dp), where mu is a probability measure on [0,1].
%C A400334 For fixed n, let M_n = lim_{N->infinity} (N-1)*max_{K=0..N0} inf_mu TV(Hyp(N,K,n), int_0^1 Bin(n,p) mu(dp)). Then lim_{n->infinity} M_n/n = (3/2)*exp(-6^(1/3)) (Gulati, 2026, Proposition 6.5).
%C A400334 This constant equals V_{0,3}, the limiting Poisson-boundary score of the count tranche {1,2,3}. More generally, V_{0,d} = (d/2)*exp(-(d!)^(1/d)) is the limiting score of {1,...,d}, uniquely maximized at d=3.
%C A400334 For the sharp first-order constant M_n, the maximizing count tranche, up to reflection, is {1,2} for 4 <= n <= 76, {3,4,5,6} for 77 <= n <= 341, and {1,2,3} for n >= 342 (Gulati, 2026, Corollary 6.6).
%C A400334 A transcendental number by Lindemann-Weierstrass theorem. - _Jianing Song_, Sep 23 2026
%H A400334 Rick Gulati, <a href="https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.7360621">The Nearest Mixture to Sampling Without Replacement Is a Single Coin</a>, SSRN 7360621, 2026, Prop. 6.5 and Cor. 6.6.
%H A400334 <a href="/index/Tra#transcendental">Index entries for transcendental numbers</a>.
%F A400334 Equals lim_{n->oo} M_n/n, where for n >= 342, M_n = (3*n/2)*(1+t_n)^(1-n) and t_n = (6/((n-1)*(n-2)*(n-3)))^(1/3).
%e A400334 0.2437394420463388257959593362016428943291145854004487898...
%t A400334 RealDigits[3/2 Exp[-6^(1/3)], 10, 105][[1]]
%o A400334 (Python) from mpmath import mp, mpf, exp, cbrt; mp.dps=110; print(mpf(3)/2*exp(-cbrt(6)))
